A||||7||PAUL88GT||Replacing OEM electrical connector?||||5||
Z||000000||PAUL88GT||05-23-2001||12:59 PM||||My IAC electrical connector on the wiring harness got burned (melted some) on the exhaust. Car is v6 86SE. Is there any way to remove the pins from the connector and replace just the plastic part of the connector?  It looks like you would need a special tool to remove the wires and pins. If so, I need the part number for the IAC connector housing.  I do not want to splice in another complete connector from a parts car, but will if I have to.  TIA.<P>Paul ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Taijiguy||05-23-2001||01:13 PM||||I've got a connector you can have n/c. If you can pull the pins from the one you have now, you can pull them from a donor. Don't know if it's possible, but either way, take this one if you want it.<BR>~Taiji||||reg||1
Z||000002||rogergarrison||05-23-2001||02:02 PM||||i rather replace whole thing, little bit of solder and shrink wrap is good as new, almost undetectable.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||05-23-2001||02:18 PM||||That connector and many others are available as prefab parts in MotorMite's ConnectTite line. You should be able to get it thru most parts stores.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||cadaver||05-23-2001||11:12 PM||||Yes its a pin extraction tool. There are many different types. I probably have one but without looking at the connector I cant say what it looks like. They are usually pretty easy to make.||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||05-24-2001||11:15 AM||||The pin extractor for Weather-Pack is a round tube that slides over the end of the pin/socket. Same tool fits male and female.<P>You can get the tools thru MotorMite or Lisle. I like the Lisle tool better. It fits several things besides Weather-Pack. <P>you can view lisle catalog online but you have to get stuff thru a car parts shop. <A HREF="http://www.lislecorp.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.lislecorp.com</A>  the terminal tool is # 14900||||reg||1
Z||000006||PAUL88GT||05-24-2001||12:22 PM||||This forum is great.  Thanks to everyone.  Taijiguy: I will take you up on that IAC connector.  That will save me from having to search the junkyards and cut one off a good harness. You have e-mail.<BR>Ogre: you are a walking data base. That tool is about $14, but I do not have a dealer in my city according to the web site. I may have to order it.<BR>Paul||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||05-24-2001||04:03 PM||||I've managed to locate a source or 2 for nearly every terminal/connector and the tools for them. It took awhile. Hardest part was finding someone who caries a good crimper. (If those terminals aren't crimped right they fall off in no time...)<P>Call the parts stores in you area. Ask them if they carry Lisle tools. Not all the places that carry it get their stuff directly from Lisle and often aren't listed online...<P>You may still have to order it, but at least it's worth calling around and seeing if someone has it. I don't believe Lisle accepts direct sales orders from end users. You'll have to use a store that carries it.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 05-24-2001).]||||reg||1
